.TH NEOXD 1 "September 2026" "neoxd" "User Manual"
.SH NAME
neoxd \- hex dumper and analyzer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B neoxd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR|\fB\-\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B neoxd
is a modern replacement for standard UNIX hex dumpers like \fBxxd\fR and \fBhexdump\fR.
It is to-date, feature-full hex dumper and binary analysis UNIX(i stress that because this is surely not built for Windows as it specifically uses UNIX-specific modules) utility written in Nim, engineered as a modern direct replacement for traditional UNIX tools such as `xxd` and `hexdump` and also most hex dumpers which claims to be replacemnets for the traditional ones. While xxd and other dumpers present raw binary data as a static, uniform grid of hex bytes, *neoxd* treats binary data as a structured, dynamic information stream. It pairs raw inspection with real-time statistical entropy analysis(using Shannon), automatic signature and file type detection, summarization,  dump reversal and restoration, and proactive CLI guardrails. It natively binds to standard C functions to automatically pipe output into a terminal pager (defaulting to `less -RFX`) if the output isn't being redirected to a file. So, it is simple enough for daily use yet it is far better utility than traditional ones.
.PP
Additionally, \fBneoxd\fR incorporates the PRUFER guardrail engine to prevent accidental file truncation or overwriting caused by inverted command-line arguments.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-cols\fR, \fB\-\-columns\fR=\fIINT\fR
Number of octets per line (default: 16)
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-window\fR=\fIINT\fR
Set the sliding window size for entropy calculation (default: 256)
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-summary\fR
Scan the target file and output a comprehensive file summary report, including entropy classification, structural block density, byte frequency histogram, and embedded magic headers.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Write output directly to \fIFILE\fR. Disables ANSI color codes automatically unless \fB\-\-color\fR is explicitly set.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable ANSI color escape sequences.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR
Force ANSI color escape sequences even when piping output or writing to a file.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pager\fR
Disable automatic terminal pager redirection. By default, interactive terminal output invokes the environment pager specified in \fB$PAGER\fR or falls back to \fBless \-RFX\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-min\fR, \fB\-\-min\-entropy\fR=\fIFLOAT\fR
Only output lines/blocks with a rolling entropy value greater than or equal to \fIFLOAT\fR (range: 0.0 to 8.0).
.TP
\fB\-\-max\fR, \fB\-\-max\-entropy\fR=\fIFLOAT\fR
Only output lines/blocks with a rolling entropy value less than or equal to \fIFLOAT\fR (range: 0.0 to 8.0).
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-plain\fR
Output in plain hexadecimal format (continuous hex bytes without offset addresses or ASCII representations).
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-seek\fR=\fIOFFSET\fR
Start reading at byte offset \fIOFFSET\fR. Accepts decimal values (e.g., 4096) or hexadecimal values prefixed with \fB0x\fR (e.g., 0x1000).
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-length\fR=\fIBYTES\fR
Process at most \fIBYTES\fR from the input stream.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\fR
Reverse a hex dump stream back into raw binary bytes.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-restore\fR
Restore a malformed or heavily modified hex dump back into a clean formatted dump representation.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-magic\fR=\fIFILE\fR
Load custom magic byte signatures from a CSV file (Format: \fIHEX_BYTES,Label\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-nop\fR, \fB\-\-no\-prufer\fR
Bypass PRUFER argument validation, flag typo suggestions, and safety checks.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-debug\fR
Enable debug diagnostics and print detailed error logs to \fIstderr\fR.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show built-in usage instructions and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print program version and license info.
.SH SAFETY GUARDS (PRUFER)
\fBneoxd\fR includes the PRUFER validation subsystem enabled by default to prevent catastrophic data destruction:
.IP \(bu 3
\fBTypo Detection:\fR Catches invalid option flags and suggests corrections using Levenshtein distance matching.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBFile Swap Prevention:\fR Aborts execution if an existing target file is passed as an output argument while the input file does not exist.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBBinary Target Protection:\fR Blocks writing raw hex text directly into known binary extensions (.bin, .elf, .exe, .png, etc.) unless restore mode is active.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBPAGER\fR
Determines the terminal pager command used for interactive output. Defaults to \fBless \-RFX\fR if unset.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect a firmware file using 32 octets per line:
.RS
.B neoxd \-c 32 firmware.bin
.RE
.PP
Show only packed or encrypted blocks with high entropy:
.RS
.B neoxd \-\-min=7.2 target.bin
.RE
.PP
Generate a structural distribution summary report:
.RS
.B neoxd \-s binary.elf
.RE
.PP
Safely write a hex dump to a file without risk of source file destruction:
.RS
.B neoxd \-o dump.txt image.jpg
.RE
.PP
Reconstruct binary data from stdin in plain hex format:
.RS
.B cat dump.hex | neoxd \-r \-p > original.bin
.RE
